WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.673
Euclid's Elements, Book One, Proposition Forty-Eight.

2
00:00:03.673 --> 00:00:12.390
If the square on one side of a triangle equals the sum of the squares on the other two sides, the angle contained by those two sides is right.

3
00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:18.651
In triangle A B C, let the square on segment B C equal the sum of the squares on segments B A and A C.

4
00:00:18.651 --> 00:00:21.290
We prove that angle B A C is a right angle.

5
00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:27.491
By Proposition Eleven, from point A draw segment A D at right angles to segment A C.

6
00:00:27.491 --> 00:00:35.680
By Proposition Three, make segment A D equal to segment B A, and join segment D C, forming right-angled triangle A D C.

7
00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:49.391
Since segment D A equals segment A B, the square on D A equals the square on A B; adding the square on A C to each, the squares on D A and A C equal the squares on B A and A C.

8
00:00:49.391 --> 00:01:03.210
Because angle D A C is right, by Proposition Forty-Seven, the square on D C equals the squares on D A and A C, while by hypothesis the square on B C equals the squares on B A and A C.

9
00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:09.025
Thus the square on D C equals the square on B C, so side D C equals side B C.

10
00:01:09.025 --> 00:01:19.219
Since side D A equals side A B, side A C is common, and base D C equals base B C, by Proposition Eight, angle D A C equals angle B A C.

11
00:01:19.219 --> 00:01:26.620
Because angle D A C is a right angle, angle B A C is also a right angle, as was required to prove.
